The Java algorithm includes a method for checking if a number is prime, then a main method to loop through numbers from 1 to 100 and display the primes ones.

Here is a basic algorithm:

```java
import java.awt.*;
import javax.swing.*;

public class PrimeNumbers extends JFrame {

    private JTextArea area;

    public PrimeNumbers() {

        setLayout(new FlowLayout());
        setSize(250, 350);
        setTitle("Prime Numbers");
        setDefaultCloseOperation(EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        area = new JTextArea(15, 20);
        JScrollPane pane = new JScrollPane(area);
        add(pane);

        StringBuilder primeNumbers = new StringBuilder();
        for(int i = 1; i <= 100; i++){
            if(isPrime(i)){
                primeNumbers.append(i).append(" ");
            }
        }

        area.setText(primeNumbers.toString());
        area.setCaretPosition(0);
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i < Math.sqrt(n) + 1; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                new PrimeNumbers().setVisible(true);
            }
        });
    }
}
```

This program uses Swing to create a GUI. The GUI has a JTextArea that will display all prime numbers from 1 to 100. The JTextArea is housed within a JScrollPane for scrolling through the prime numbers if the list extends outside of the window's viewing area. The isPrime(int) method checks if a given integer is a prime number or not. The method returns false if a number is less than or equal to one or if it is divisible by a number other than 1 or itself. Otherwise, it returns true. The main method traverses numbers 1 through 100 and checks via the isPrime(int) method if a given number is a prime number or not. If it is a prime number, the number is appended to JTextArea. The caret is then set to the beginning of the JTextArea to allow scrolling from the top of the list when the window is viewed.

Note: The SwingUtilities.invokeLater is used to make sure that the GUI creation is threadsafe.

