Here is a simple Java program that uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to find all prime numbers between 1 and 100. It then prints them out.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean primes[] = new boolean[101];
        for (int i = 2; i <= 100; i++) {
            primes[i] = true;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i * i <= 100; i++) {
            if (primes[i]) {
                for (int j = i * i; j <= 100; j += i) {
                    primes[j] = false;
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Prime numbers between 1 and 100:");
        for (int i = 2; i <= 100; i++) {
            if (primes[i]) {
                System.out.print(i + " ");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This program first initializes an array of size 101 with all values set to true (indicating that they are initially assumed to be prime). Then it iterates over each number from 2 to the square root of 100 (which is 10), checking if the number is marked as prime. If it is, then all multiples of this number are marked as non-prime. Finally, it prints out all numbers that were marked as prime.

To visualize these prime numbers, you could use a graphical library like JavaFX or Swing to create a GUI where the prime numbers are displayed in a list or on a number line. However, this would require additional code beyond what's shown here.

